hadiths[140]{hadithnumber,text}:
"18637","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ said that Paradise is built of a brick of silver and a brick of gold, and its mortar is musk. > **""Paradise is a brick of silver and a brick of gold, and its mortar is musk.""**"
"18638","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that when Allah created the Garden of Eden, He created therein what no eye has seen, no ear has heard, and what has never crossed the heart of a mortal, then He said to it, speak, and it said, the believers have succeeded. > **""When Allah created the Garden of Eden, He created therein what no eye has seen, no ear has heard, and what has never crossed the heart of a mortal.""**"
"18639","In another narration, Allah created the Garden of Eden with His Hand, hung its fruits therein and split its rivers therein, then looked at it and said to it, speak, and it said, the believers have succeeded. Then Allah said, by My might, no miser shall dwell beside Me in you. > **""By My might, no miser shall dwell beside Me in you.""**"
"18640","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Prophet ﷺ was asked about Paradise and said that whoever enters Paradise lives therein and never dies, enjoys bliss and never despairs, his garments never wear out and his youth never perishes. When asked what it is built of, he said a brick of gold and a brick of silver, its mortar musk, its soil saffron, and its pebbles pearls and rubies. > **""Whoever enters Paradise lives therein and never dies, enjoys bliss and never despairs, his garments never wear out and his youth never perishes.""**"
"18641","Abu Saʿid, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that Allah, Blessed and Exalted is He, created Paradise, a brick of gold and a brick of silver, and its mortar is musk. > **""Allah created Paradise, a brick of gold and a brick of silver, and its mortar is musk.""**"
"18642","In another narration, the wall of Paradise is a brick of gold and a brick of silver, its mortar musk, and Allah said to it, speak, and it said, the believers have succeeded, so the angels said, blessed are you, abode of kings. In another wording, Allah created the Garden of Eden with His Hand, a brick of gold and a brick of silver, and the rest is similar. > **""The believers have succeeded, so blessed are you, abode of kings.""**"
"18643","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Allah created Paradise white. > **""Allah created Paradise white.""**"
"18644","Abu Saʿid al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that between the two leaves of a gate in Paradise is a journey of forty years. > **""Between the two leaves of a gate in Paradise is a journey of forty years.""**"
"18645","Muʿawiyah ibn Haydah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that they complete seventy nations, being the last and most honoured of them before Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, and between the two leaves of a gate of Paradise is forty years, and a day will come upon it when it is crowded. > **""You complete seventy nations, being the last and most honoured before Allah, and between the two leaves of a gate of Paradise is forty years.""**"
"18646","ʿAbdullah ibn Salam, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that between the two leaves in Paradise is forty years, and a day will come when it is thronged as camels throng, returning thirsty on the fifth day. > **""Between the two leaves in Paradise is forty years, and a day will come when it is thronged as thirsty camels throng.""**"
"18647","Abu Musa, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ said the gardens of al-Firdaws are four, and then the narrator mentioned the full hadith. > **""The gardens of al-Firdaws are four.""**"
"18648","Samurah, meaning Ibn Jundub, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that al-Firdaws is the hill of Paradise, its highest and its middle, and from it burst the rivers of Paradise. In an addition, if you ask Allah then ask Him for al-Firdaws. > **""Al-Firdaws is the hill of Paradise, its highest and its middle, and from it burst the rivers of Paradise.""**"
"18649","Al-ʿIrbad ibn Sariyah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that if they ask Allah, let them ask Him for al-Firdaws. > **""If you ask Allah, ask Him for al-Firdaws.""**"
"18650","Samurah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say to them that al-Firdaws is the middle hill of Paradise, its highest and most beautiful. > **""Al-Firdaws is the middle hill of Paradise, its highest and most beautiful.""**"
"18651","Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that they should ask Allah for al-Firdaws, for it is the navel of Paradise, and the people of al-Firdaws hear the creaking of the Throne. > **""Ask Allah for al-Firdaws, for it is the navel of Paradise.""**"
"18652","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that for every people of a deed there is a gate of Paradise from which they are called by that deed, and then the narrator mentioned the full hadith. > **""For every people of a deed there is a gate of Paradise from which they are called by that deed.""**"
"18653","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that on the Day of Resurrection a man is called by his greatest deed, if prayer was best he is called by it, if fasting then by it, if jihad then by it, then he comes to a gate of Paradise called al-Rayyan from which the fasting are called. Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, asked whether anyone is called by two deeds, and he said yes, you. > **""On the Day of Resurrection a man is called by his greatest deed, and the fasting are called from a gate called al-Rayyan.""**"
"18654","Salman al-Farisi, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that none enters Paradise without a pass: in the Name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate, this is a writ from Allah for so-and-so son of so-and-so, enter a lofty Paradise whose clusters hang near. > **""None enters Paradise without a pass: enter a lofty Paradise whose clusters hang near.""**"
"18655","Muʿadh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he asked the Prophet ﷺ, or heard him saying, that the people of Paradise enter Paradise hairless, beardless, kohl-eyed, thirty years old. > **""The people of Paradise enter Paradise hairless, beardless, kohl-eyed, thirty years old.""**"
"18656","In another narration, the believers are raised on the Day of Resurrection hairless, beardless, kohl-eyed, thirty years old. > **""The believers are raised hairless, beardless, kohl-eyed, thirty years old.""**"
"18657","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the people of Paradise enter Paradise hairless, beardless, kohl-eyed. > **""The people of Paradise enter Paradise hairless, beardless, kohl-eyed.""**"
"18658","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the people of Paradise enter Paradise hairless, beardless, white, curly-haired, kohl-eyed, thirty-three years old, upon the form of Adam, sixty cubits by seven cubits. > **""The people of Paradise enter Paradise hairless, beardless, kohl-eyed, thirty-three years old, upon the form of Adam.""**"
"18659","Abu Saʿid, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, with doubt from Abu Khaythamah, that whoever dies of the people of this world, young or old, is returned to sixty years in Paradise, never exceeding it, and likewise the people of the Fire. > **""Whoever dies, young or old, is returned to sixty years in Paradise, never exceeding it.""**"
"18660","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that every person of the Fire sees his seat in Paradise and says, if only Allah had guided me, so it becomes a regret upon him, and every person of Paradise sees his seat in the Fire and says, were it not that Allah guided me, so it becomes gratitude for him. > **""Every person of the Fire sees his seat in Paradise and says, if only Allah had guided me.""**"
"18661","In another narration, none enters the Fire but he sees his seat in Paradise, had he done good, that it be a regret upon him, and none enters Paradise but he sees his seat in the Fire, had he done evil, that his gratitude increase. > **""None enters the Fire but he sees his seat in Paradise, that it be a regret upon him, and none enters Paradise but he sees his seat in the Fire, that his gratitude increase.""**"
"18662","Jabir ibn ʿAbdillah, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to the Jews that he would ask them about the soil of Paradise, which is white flour. When he asked them, they said it is bread, O father of al-Qasim, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said bread is from flour. > **""I shall ask them about the soil of Paradise, which is white flour.""**"
"18663","ʿUmarah ibn Khuzaymah reports that while they were with ʿAmr ibn al-ʿAs, may Allah be pleased with him, during a pilgrimage or lesser pilgrimage, ʿAmr said that while they were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in this mountain pass, the Prophet said, look, do you see anything. They said they saw crows, among them a pied crow with red beak and feet, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that none of the women enters Paradise but she who is among them like this crow among crows. > **""None of the women enters Paradise but she who is among them like this crow among crows.""**"
"18664","In another narration, they were with ʿAmr ibn al-ʿAs in a pilgrimage or lesser pilgrimage until at Marr al-Zahran there was a woman in her litter, and then he mentioned the like of it. > **""None of the women enters Paradise but she who is among them like this crow among crows.""**"
"18665","Abu Saʿid al-Khudri and Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with them both, report that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said concerning the last two men to leave the Fire: Allah says to one of them, O son of Adam, what have you prepared for this day, did you ever do good, did you ever hope in Me, and he says, no my Lord, so he is ordered to the Fire in utter regret. He says to the other, O son of Adam, what have you prepared for this day, did you ever do good, did you ever hope in Me, and he says, no my Lord, except that I hoped in You. Then a tree is raised for him and he says, O my Lord, settle me beneath this tree to shade in its shade, eat of its fruits and drink of its water, pledging not to ask for other than it, so He settles him beneath it. Then a tree better than the first with more abundant water is raised for him, and he says, O my Lord, settle me beneath it and I shall ask You for nothing else, to shade in its shade, eat of its fruit and drink of its water. Allah says, O son of Adam, did you not pledge not to ask Me for other than it, and he says, my Lord, this one and I shall ask for nothing else, so He settles him beneath it with a pledge. Then a tree at the gate of Paradise, better than the first two with more abundant water, is raised for him, and he says, O my Lord, settle me beneath it to shade in its shade, eat of its fruit and drink of its water, and Allah says, O son of Adam, did you not pledge not to ask Me for other than it, and he says, my Lord, this one and I shall ask for nothing else, so He draws him near beneath it with a pledge not to ask for other than it. Then he hears the voices of the people of Paradise and cannot restrain himself, saying, my Lord, admit me to Paradise. Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, says, ask and wish, so he asks and wishes for the measure of three days of this world, Allah teaching him what he knows not, and when he finishes He says, son of Adam, yours is what you asked. Abu Saʿid said, and the like thereof with it, and Abu Hurayrah said, and ten times the like thereof with it, and each told the other to narrate what he heard. > **""Ask and wish, so he asks and wishes, and his Lord says, yours is what you asked and the like thereof with it.""**"
"18666","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the lowest of the people of Paradise in station has seven degrees, he upon the sixth with the seventh above him, three hundred servants, morning and evening brought three hundred platters of gold, each with what is not in the other, relishing the first as he relishes the last, saying, O my Lord, if You permitted me I would feed the people of Paradise and give them drink without diminishing what is with me, and of the wide-eyed maidens seventy-two wives besides his own wives, each taking a seat a mile of earth. > **""The lowest of the people of Paradise in station has seven degrees, three hundred servants, and seventy-two wives of the wide-eyed maidens.""**"
"18667","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the lowest of the people of Paradise in fortune are a people Allah brings out of the Fire, their Lord showing mercy to them as they associated nothing with Allah. They appear in the open and sprout as herbs sprout, until when the souls enter their bodies they say, our Lord, as You brought us out of the Fire and returned the souls to our bodies, turn our faces away from the Fire, so He turns their faces away from the Fire. > **""Our Lord, as You brought us out of the Fire, turn our faces away from the Fire.""**"
"18668","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that if the adornment of the least of the people of Paradise were weighed against all the people of this world, what Allah gives him in the Hereafter would be better than the adornment of all the people of this world. > **""If the adornment of the least of the people of Paradise were weighed against all this world, what Allah gives him in the Hereafter would be better.""**"
"18669","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Prophet ﷺ said that the lowest of the people of Paradise in station looks over his dominion for two thousand years, seeing its farthest as its nearest, looking upon his wives and servants. > **""The lowest of the people of Paradise in station looks over his dominion for two thousand years, seeing its farthest as its nearest.""**"
"18670","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying that the lowest of all the people of Paradise in degree has ten thousand standing at his head, in the hand of each two platters, one of gold and one of silver, each with a dish not like the other, eating of the last as he eats of the first, finding in the last the same fragrance and delight as the first, then it becomes the scent of strong musk. They neither urinate nor defecate nor blow their noses, brethren upon couches facing one another. > **""The lowest of the people of Paradise in degree has ten thousand standing at his head, in the hand of each two platters, one of gold and one of silver.""**"
"18671","ʿAwf ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he knew the last of the people of Paradise to enter: a man who used to say, O Allah, remove me from the Fire, without saying admit me to Paradise. When the people of Paradise enter Paradise and the people of the Fire enter the Fire, that man remains and says, O my Lord, why am I here. Allah says, that is what you asked Me, O son of Adam. He says, O my Lord, bring me near Paradise. Allah says, O son of Adam, you never asked Me. Then Allah raises for him a tree at the gate of Paradise, and he says, O my Lord, bring me near this tree to eat of its fruit and shade in its shade. Allah says, O son of Adam, did you not ask Me only to remove you from the Fire. He keeps asking until it is said to him, go, yours is what your feet reach and your eyes see. > **""O Allah, remove me from the Fire.""**"
"18672","Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the last man to enter Paradise is one turning upon the bridge on back and belly, like a boy his father beats while he flees, unable by his deeds to hasten. He says, O my Lord, convey me to Paradise and save me from the Fire. Allah reveals to him, My servant, if I save you from the Fire and admit you to Paradise, will you confess to Me your sins and errors. The servant says, yes my Lord, by Your might and majesty, if You save me from the Fire I shall confess my sins and errors. He crosses the bridge, then says to himself, if I confess my sins and errors He will return me to the Fire. Allah reveals to him, My servant, confess to Me your sins and errors and I shall forgive them and admit you to Paradise. The servant says, nay, by Your might, I never committed a sin nor erred at all. Allah reveals to him, My servant, I have proof against you. The servant looks right and left, seeing no one, and says, O my Lord, show me Your proof. Allah makes his skin speak of minor sins, and seeing that the servant says, O my Lord, by Your might I have hidden ones. Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, reveals to him, My servant, I know them better than you, confess them and I shall forgive them and admit you to Paradise. The servant confesses his sins and He admits him to Paradise. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ laughed until his molars appeared, saying, this is the lowest of the people of Paradise in station, so how of those above him. > **""My servant, confess to Me your sins and errors and I shall forgive them and admit you to Paradise.""**"
"18673","Ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the last of the people of Paradise to enter is a man whose Lord, the Mighty and Majestic, passes by him and says to him, rise and enter Paradise. He turns to Him frowning and says, have You left anything for me. He says, yes, yours is the like of all the sun has risen and set upon. > **""Yours is the like of all the sun has risen and set upon.""**"
"18674","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ said that most of the people of Paradise are the simple-minded. > **""Most of the people of Paradise are the simple-minded.""**"
"18675","Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he heard the Prophet ﷺ saying that he hoped those of his nation who followed him on the Day of Resurrection would be a quarter of the people of Paradise, so they magnified Allah. Then he said he hoped they would be a third of mankind, so they magnified Allah, then he said he hoped they would be half. > **""I hope those of my nation who follow me will be a quarter of the people of Paradise, then a third, then half.""**"
"18676","Abu Musa, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that the people of Paradise are a hundred and twenty rows, eighty of them his nation and forty the rest of the nations. > **""The people of Paradise are a hundred and twenty rows, eighty of them my nation and forty the rest of the nations.""**"
"18677","Ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to them, how would you be with a quarter of Paradise, yours a quarter and the rest of mankind three quarters. They said Allah and His Messenger know best. He said, how would you be with a third. They said that is more. He said, how would you be with half. They said that is more. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the people of Paradise on the Day of Resurrection are a hundred and twenty rows, eighty of them them. > **""The people of Paradise on the Day of Resurrection are a hundred and twenty rows, eighty of them you.""**"
"18678","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that the people of Paradise are a hundred and twenty rows, eighty of them his nation. > **""The people of Paradise are a hundred and twenty rows, eighty of them my nation.""**"
"18679","Muʿawiyah ibn Haydah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that the people of Paradise are a hundred and twenty rows, eighty of them them and the rest of that mankind. > **""The people of Paradise are a hundred and twenty rows, eighty of them you and the rest mankind.""**"
"18680","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that they are a third of the people of Paradise, or half of the people of Paradise. > **""You are a third of the people of Paradise, or half of the people of Paradise.""**"
"18681","ʿAli ibn Khalid reports that Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, passed by Khalid ibn Yazid ibn Muʿawiyah, who asked him for the gentlest word he heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Abu Umamah said he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying, verily all of you enter Paradise except one who strays from Allah as a camel strays from its folk. > **""Verily all of you enter Paradise except one who strays from Allah as a camel strays from its folk.""**"
"18682","Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, said that none of this nation remains but enters Paradise except one who strays from Allah as an evil camel strays from its folk, and whoever does not believe him, then Allah, Exalted is He, says, none shall enter it but the most wretched, who denied and turned away, denying what Muhammad ﷺ brought and turning from it. > **""None of this nation remains but enters Paradise except one who strays from Allah as a camel strays from its folk.""**"
"18683","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that they shall all enter Paradise, every one all together, except one who strays from Allah as a camel strays. > **""You shall all enter Paradise, every one all together, except one who strays from Allah as a camel strays.""**"
"18684","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ what his Lord answered him regarding intercession. The Prophet said, by the One in Whose Hand is the soul of Muhammad, he thought he would be the first of his nation to ask him that for the eagerness for knowledge he saw in him. By the One in Whose Hand is the soul of Muhammad, their thronging at the gates of Paradise concerns him no more than the completion of his intercession, and his intercession is for whoever testifies there is no god but Allah sincerely, his heart confirming his tongue and his tongue his heart. > **""My intercession is for whoever testifies there is no god but Allah sincerely, his heart confirming his tongue and his tongue his heart.""**"
"18685","Abu Malik, meaning al-Ashʿari, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, swearing by the One in Whose Hand is his soul, that Allah shall send forth of them on the Day of Resurrection to Paradise a company all together like the black night, trampling the earth, and the angels shall say, those who came with Muhammad are more than those who came with the prophets. > **""By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, Allah shall send forth of you to Paradise a company like the black night, and those who came with Muhammad are more than those who came with the prophets.""**"
"18686","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ said his Lord, the Mighty and Majestic, promised him to admit a hundred thousand of his nation to Paradise. Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said, increase us, O Messenger of Allah, and he said, and like this, gesturing with his hand. Abu Bakr said, O Prophet of Allah, increase us, and he said, and like this. ʿUmar said, enough, O Abu Bakr, and Abu Bakr said, what is it to you, O son of al-Khattab. ʿUmar said, Allah if He wills can admit all mankind to Paradise with a single handful. The Prophet ﷺ said, ʿUmar spoke the truth. > **""My Lord promised me to admit a hundred thousand of my nation to Paradise.""**"
"18687","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, promised him to admit four hundred thousand of his nation to Paradise. Abu Bakr said, increase us, O Messenger of Allah, and he said, and like this, joining his palms, and then he mentioned the like of it. > **""Allah promised me to admit four hundred thousand of my nation to Paradise.""**"
"18688","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that seventy thousand of his nation enter Paradise. They said, increase us, O Messenger of Allah, and he said, with each man seventy thousand. They said, increase us, O Messenger of Allah, and he was upon a mound and scooped with his hands. They said, increase us, O Messenger of Allah, and he said, this, scooping with his hands. They said, O Prophet of Allah, Allah keep far whoever enters the Fire after this. > **""Seventy thousand of my nation enter Paradise, and with each man seventy thousand.""**"
"18689","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he asked his Lord, the Mighty and Majestic, Who promised him to admit seventy thousand of his nation to Paradise in the image of the moon on the night of fullness. He asked for more and He gave him with each thousand seventy thousand. He said, O my Lord, if these are not the emigrants of my nation. He said, then I shall complete them for you from the Bedouins. > **""My Lord promised me to admit seventy thousand of my nation to Paradise in the image of the moon on the night of fullness.""**"
"18690","Asmaʾ bint Abi Bakr, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the sun eclipsed in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and she heard the clamour of the people saying a sign, while they were at Fariʿ. She went out wrapped in a quilt of al-Zubayr until she entered upon ʿAʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood praying with the people. Then she mentioned the hadith until he said, and I saw fifty or seventy thousand entering Paradise in the image of the moon on the night of fullness. A man stood and said, supplicate Allah to make me of them, and he said, O Allah, make him of them. O people, you shall not ask me of anything until I descend but I shall inform you of it. Then a man stood and said, who is my father. He said, your father is so-and-so, the one to whom he was ascribed. > **""I saw fifty or seventy thousand entering Paradise in the image of the moon on the night of fullness.""**"
"18691","Abu Bakr ibn ʿUmayr, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ said that Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, promised him to admit three hundred thousand of his nation to Paradise. ʿUmayr said, O Prophet of Allah, increase us, and he said, like this. ʿUmayr said, O Prophet of Allah, increase us, and ʿUmar said, enough, O ʿUmayr. He said, what is it to you, O son of al-Khattab, what is it to you if Allah admits us to Paradise. ʿUmar said, Allah if He wills admits mankind to Paradise with a single handful or scoop. The Prophet ﷺ said, ʿUmar spoke the truth. > **""Allah promised me to admit three hundred thousand of my nation to Paradise.""**"
"18692","ʿAbdullah ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that they spoke at length with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ one night, then came to him in the morning and he said that the prophets with their nations were shown to him that night. A prophet passed with three, a prophet with a band, a prophet with a company, a prophet with none, until Musa, peace be upon him, passed with a multitude of the Children of Israel, pleasing him. He asked who they were, and it was said, this is your brother Musa with the Children of Israel. He asked, where is my nation. It was said, look to your right, and the horizon was filled with the faces of men. It was said, are you pleased. He said, I am pleased, my Lord. It was said, with these are seventy thousand who enter Paradise without reckoning. The Prophet ﷺ said, may my father and mother be ransom for you, if you can be of the seventy thousand then do so, and if you fall short then be of the people of the hills, and if you fall short then be of the people of the horizon, for I saw there people mingling. ʿUkashah ibn Mihsan stood and said, supplicate Allah for me, O Messenger of Allah, to make me of the seventy, so he supplicated for him. Another man stood and said, supplicate Allah, O Messenger of Allah, to make me of them, and he said, ʿUkashah has preceded you in it. Then they discussed who the seventy thousand are, saying a people born in Islam who never associated anything with Allah until they died. That reached the Prophet ﷺ, who said they are those who do not cauterise, nor seek ruqyah, nor take omens, and upon their Lord they rely. > **""With these are seventy thousand who enter Paradise without reckoning, those who do not cauterise, nor seek ruqyah, nor take omens, and upon their Lord they rely.""**"
"18693","Jabir ibn ʿAbdillah, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ delayed the noon prayer to the end of its time, then came out and prayed, then said he saw in a dream that the nations were shown to him. A prophet came with five or more, then he saw a great multitude and thought it his nation, but it was said this is the nation of Musa. He saw ʿIsa son of Maryam, white, curly, inclining to redness, then he saw, mentioning words whose meaning was a great number, and it was said it is his nation, and that with them are seventy thousand who enter Paradise without reckoning or punishment. ʿUkashah al-Asadi said, O Messenger of Allah, make me among these seventy, and he said, you are of them. Another said, O Messenger of Allah, make me of them, and he said, ʿUkashah has preceded you in it. The people asked who the seventy are, some saying those whose hearts softened to Islam, others a people of believers who never associated nor worshipped aught but Allah, raising their voices. The Prophet ﷺ came out and asked what these voices are. They said, O Messenger of Allah, the seventy you mentioned, who are they. He said they are those who do not cauterise, nor seek ruqyah, nor take omens, and upon their Lord they rely. > **""With them are seventy thousand who enter Paradise without reckoning or punishment, those who do not cauterise, nor seek ruqyah, nor take omens, and upon their Lord they rely.""**"
"18694","Abu Ayyub, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out to them one day and said to them that his Lord, the Mighty and Majestic, gave him the choice between seventy thousand entering Paradise without reckoning and the stored thing with Him for his nation. One of his Companions asked, O Messenger of Allah, will your Lord store that. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered, then came out magnifying Allah, saying his Lord increased him with each thousand seventy thousand, and the stored thing with Him. Then he mentioned the hadith, which is cited in intercession. > **""My Lord gave me the choice between seventy thousand entering Paradise without reckoning and the stored intercession for my nation, then increased me with each thousand seventy thousand.""**"
"18695","Jabir ibn ʿAbdillah, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was late one night for the evening prayer until much of the night had gone and some in the mosque slept. He came out with people between sleeping and praying awaiting the prayer, and said that people remain in prayer so long as they await it, and were it not for the weakness of the old and the weeping of the young he would have delayed the evening prayer to the depth of night. Then he said seventy thousand enter Paradise with no reckoning upon them. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered, and when he entered they discussed the seventy among themselves, whether they are the martyrs or the believers. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out and asked what they discussed. They informed him, and he said they are those who do not cauterise, nor seek ruqyah, nor take omens, and upon their Lord they rely. > **""Seventy thousand enter Paradise with no reckoning upon them, those who do not cauterise, nor seek ruqyah, nor take omens, and upon their Lord they rely.""**"
"18696","Shurayh ibn ʿUbayd reports that Thawban, may Allah be pleased with him, fell ill at Hims under its governor ʿAbdullah ibn Qurt al-Azdi, who did not visit him. A man of the Kalaʿis came visiting Thawban, who asked whether he could write. He said yes, so Thawban dictated for the commander ʿAbdullah ibn Qurt: from Thawban, freedman of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, to proceed, had Musa and ʿIsa, peace be upon them, a freedman in your presence you would have visited him. Then he folded the letter and told him to deliver it, which he did to Ibn Qurt, who upon reading it rose alarmed. The people asked what was wrong, whether something new occurred. Ibn Qurt came to Thawban, visited him and sat with him a while, then rose, but Thawban seized his cloak and said sit until I narrate a hadith I heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: seventy thousand of my nation shall enter Paradise with no reckoning upon them nor punishment, with each thousand seventy thousand. > **""Seventy thousand of my nation shall enter Paradise with no reckoning upon them nor punishment, with each thousand seventy thousand.""**"
"18697","Abu Saʿid, meaning al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said seventy thousand of his nation enter Paradise with no reckoning upon them. ʿUkashah stood and said, O Messenger of Allah, supplicate Allah to make me of them, and he said, O Allah, make him of them. The people fell silent, then said to one another, had we said, O Messenger of Allah, supplicate Allah to make us of them. He said, ʿUkashah and his companion have preceded you in it, but had you said I would have said, and had I said it would have been binding. > **""Seventy thousand of my nation enter Paradise with no reckoning upon them.""**"
"18698","Al-Falatan ibn ʿAsim, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ was in a gathering when his gaze fixed upon a man walking in the mosque. He said, O so-and-so, and he answered, at your service, O Messenger of Allah, speaking nothing else but that. The Prophet asked, do you testify that I am the Messenger of Allah. He said no. The Prophet asked, do you read the Torah. He said yes. And the Gospel. He said yes. And the Qurʾan. He said, by the One in Whose Hand is my soul, had I wished I could read it. Then he adjured him, do you find me in the Torah and the Gospel. He said they find his likeness, his emergence and his appearance, and hoped it would be among them, but when he emerged they feared he was he, then looked and found he was not he. The Prophet asked why. He said with him of his nation are seventy thousand with no reckoning nor punishment, while with him is but a small company. The Prophet said, by the One in Whose Hand is my soul, I am indeed he, and they are indeed my nation, and they are indeed more than seventy thousand and seventy thousand. > **""By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, I am indeed he, and they are indeed my nation, and they are indeed more than seventy thousand and seventy thousand.""**"
"18699","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that seventy thousand of his nation enter Paradise without reckoning, those who do not cauterise, nor brand, nor seek ruqyah, nor take omens, and upon their Lord they rely. > **""Seventy thousand of my nation enter Paradise without reckoning, those who do not cauterise, nor seek ruqyah, nor take omens, and upon their Lord they rely.""**"
"18700","Rifaʿah ibn ʿArabah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that they set out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when people began seeking his permission to leave and he permitted them. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, what is it with the side of the tree toward the Messenger of Allah more hateful to you than the other side. None of the people was seen but weeping. Abu Bakr said that whoever seeks his permission after this is in his view foolish. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood, praised Allah and extolled Him, saying, I bear witness before Allah, and when he swore he said, by the One in Whose Hand is the soul of Muhammad, none of you who believes in Allah then remains upright but shall be led in Paradise, and my Lord has promised me to admit of my nation seventy thousand to Paradise with no reckoning upon them nor punishment, and I hope they shall not enter it until you and the righteous of your wives and offspring take dwellings in Paradise. Then he mentioned the hadith. > **""By the One in Whose Hand is the soul of Muhammad, none of you who believes in Allah then remains upright but shall be led in Paradise.""**"
"18701","ʿUmar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying that Allah shall raise from a city in Syria called Hims ninety thousand with no reckoning upon them, between the olives and the wall and the red mound. > **""Allah shall raise from a city in Syria called Hims ninety thousand with no reckoning upon them.""**"
"18702","Sahl ibn Saʿd, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he heard the Prophet ﷺ saying that in the loins of the loins of the loins of men of his Companions are men and women who enter Paradise without reckoning, then he recited, and others of them who have not yet joined them, and He is the Mighty, the Wise. > **""In the loins of the loins of the loins of men of my Companions are men and women who enter Paradise without reckoning.""**"
"18703","Samurah ibn Jundub, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say that of his nation is a nation of whom Allah shall admit seventy thousand to Paradise without reckoning. > **""Of my nation is a nation of whom Allah shall admit seventy thousand to Paradise without reckoning.""**"
"18704","Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that on the Day of Resurrection a company of men shall stand filling the horizon, their light like the sun, and it is said, the unlettered prophet, so every prophet strains toward it, then it is said, Muhammad and his nation. Then another company shall stand filling between the two horizons, their light like the moon on the night of fullness, and every prophet strains toward it, and it is said, Muhammad and his nation. Then another company shall stand filling between the horizon, their light like every star of the sun, and it is said, the unlettered prophet, so every prophet strains toward it, and it is said, Muhammad and his nation. Then He scoops two scoops and it is said, this for you, O Muhammad, and this from Me for you, O Muhammad, then the Balance is set and the reckoning taken. > **""On the Day of Resurrection a company shall stand filling the horizon, their light like the sun, and it is said, Muhammad and his nation.""**"
"18705","Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that on the Day of Resurrection a company bright and marked shall emerge filling the horizon, their light like the light of the sun, and a caller calls, the unlettered prophet, so every unlettered prophet strains toward it, then it is said, Muhammad and his nation, and they enter Paradise with no reckoning nor punishment. Then another company bright and marked shall emerge, their light like the light of the moon on the night of fullness, filling the horizon, and a caller calls, the unlettered prophet, so every unlettered prophet strains toward it, then it is said, Muhammad and his nation, and they enter Paradise without reckoning nor punishment. Then another company bright and marked shall emerge, their light like the greatest star in the sky, filling the horizon, and a caller calls, the unlettered prophet, so every unlettered prophet strains toward it, then it is said, Muhammad and his nation, and they enter Paradise without reckoning nor punishment. Then your Lord, the Mighty and Majestic, comes, and the Balance and reckoning are set. > **""They enter Paradise without reckoning nor punishment, then your Lord comes and the Balance and reckoning are set.""**"
"18706","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that the nations were shown to him, and then he mentioned the hadith, until ʿUkashah ibn Mihsan stood and said, am I of them, O Messenger of Allah. He said yes, then another stood and said, am I of them, O Messenger of Allah. He said yes, and then he mentioned the hadith. > **""The nations were shown to me, and ʿUkashah stood and said, am I of them, and I said yes.""**"
"18707","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said seventy thousand of his nation enter Paradise without reckoning. Abu Bakr said, O Messenger of Allah, increase us, and he said, and like this. ʿUmar said, O Abu Bakr, if Allah wills He admits them to Paradise with a single handful. > **""Seventy thousand of my nation enter Paradise without reckoning.""**"
"18708","ʿUtbah ibn ʿAbd, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that his Lord promised him to admit seventy thousand of his nation to Paradise without reckoning, then each thousand intercedes for seventy thousand, and then he mentioned the hadith, a long one concerning the description of Paradise. > **""My Lord promised me to admit seventy thousand of my nation to Paradise without reckoning, then each thousand intercedes for seventy thousand.""**"
"18709","Abu Saʿd al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that his Lord promised him to admit seventy thousand of his nation to Paradise without reckoning, and each thousand intercedes for seventy thousand, then his Lord scoops three scoops with His Hands. Qays said to Abu Saʿd, did you hear this from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with your ears. He said yes, my ears heard and my heart retained it. Abu Saʿid said that, Allah willing, covers the emigrants of his nation, and Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, completes the rest from their Bedouins. > **""My Lord promised me to admit seventy thousand of my nation to Paradise without reckoning, and each thousand intercedes for seventy thousand, then my Lord scoops three scoops with His Hands.""**"
"18710","Asmaʾ bint Abi Bakr, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the sun eclipsed in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and she heard the clamour of the people saying a sign, while they were at Fariʿ. She went out wrapped in a quilt of al-Zubayr until she entered upon ʿAʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood praying for the people. Then she mentioned the hadith until he said, and I saw fifty or seventy thousand entering Paradise in the image of the moon on the night of fullness. A man stood and said, supplicate Allah to make me of them, and he said, O Allah, make him of them. O people, you shall not ask me of anything until I descend but I shall inform you of it. A man stood and said, who is my father. He said, your father is so-and-so, the one to whom he was ascribed. Then the Prophet ﷺ ascended the pulpit and said, O people, the sun and moon are two signs of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, not eclipsed for the death nor life of anyone, so when you see that hasten to prayer, charity and remembrance of Allah, and I saw of you fifty or seventy thousand entering Paradise without reckoning, and then he mentioned the like of it. > **""I saw fifty or seventy thousand entering Paradise in the image of the moon on the night of fullness, without reckoning.""**"
"18711","ʿAmir ibn ʿUmayr, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ remained three days not coming out to a prescribed prayer, and when spoken to he said he found his Lord Majestic and Generous, granting him with each one of the seventy thousand who enter Paradise without reckoning another seventy thousand. He said his nation would not reach or complete this, so Allah said He would complete them for him from the Bedouins. > **""With each one of the seventy thousand who enter Paradise without reckoning, another seventy thousand, and I shall complete them for you from the Bedouins.""**"
"18712","Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he was granted seventy thousand who enter Paradise without reckoning, their faces like the moon on the night of fullness and their hearts upon the heart of a single man. He asked his Lord, the Mighty and Majestic, for more, and He granted him with each one seventy thousand. Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said he saw that this covers the people of the villages and reaches the edges of the deserts. > **""I was granted seventy thousand who enter Paradise without reckoning, their faces like the moon on the night of fullness and their hearts upon the heart of a single man.""**"
"18713","ʿAbd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said his Lord granted him seventy thousand of his nation who enter Paradise without reckoning. ʿUmar said, O Messenger of Allah, why did you not ask Him for more. He said, I did ask Him for more and He granted me with each man seventy thousand. ʿUmar said, why did you not ask Him for more. He said, I did ask Him for more and He granted me like this, and ʿAbdullah ibn Abi Bakr parted his hands, spreading his arms and scooping, while Hisham said this is from Allah whose number none knows. > **""My Lord granted me seventy thousand of my nation who enter Paradise without reckoning, and with each man seventy thousand.""**"
"18714","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ said that when the servants stand for reckoning, a people shall come with their swords upon their necks dripping blood, crowding at the gate of Paradise. It will be said, who are these. It will be said, the martyrs who were alive and provided for. Then a caller shall call, let whoever has his reward upon Allah stand and enter Paradise, then call a third time, let whoever has his reward upon Allah stand and enter Paradise. Whoever has his reward upon Allah shall enter Paradise, so so many thousands shall stand and enter it without reckoning. > **""Let whoever has his reward upon Allah stand and enter Paradise.""**"
"18715","Abu Bakr, meaning al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that no miser enters Paradise, nor deceiver, nor traitor, nor one evil in mastery over slaves, and the first to knock at the gate of Paradise are slaves when they are upright between them and Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, and between them and their masters, and then he mentioned the hadith. > **""No miser enters Paradise, nor deceiver, nor traitor, nor one evil in mastery.""**"
"18716","Abu Saʿid al-Khudri and ʿAbdullah ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with them both, report that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the first company to enter Paradise on the Day of Resurrection have faces in the image of the moon on the night of fullness, and those after them like the most beautiful brilliant star in the sky. Each of them has two wives, upon each wife seventy garments, the marrow of her leg seen from behind the flesh as red drink is seen in a white glass. > **""The first company to enter Paradise have faces in the image of the moon on the night of fullness, and the marrow of her leg is seen from behind the flesh as red drink in a white glass.""**"
"18717","Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, says each day to Paradise, sweeten yourself for your people, so it increases in sweetness, and that is the coolness people feel at dawn from it. > **""Allah says each day to Paradise, sweeten yourself for your people.""**"
"18718","Abu Saʿid al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he heard the Prophet ﷺ saying that in Paradise is what no eye has seen, no ear has heard, and what has never crossed the heart of a mortal. > **""In Paradise is what no eye has seen, no ear has heard, and what has never crossed the heart of a mortal.""**"
"18719","Abu al-Dardaʾ, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that Allah, Blessed and Exalted is He, descends in the three hours remaining of the night, opening the remembrance in the first hour unseen by other than Him, effacing what He wills and confirming what He wills. Then He descends in the second hour to the Garden of Eden, unseen by other than Him and never crossing the heart of a mortal, wherein none of the children of Adam dwells with Him but three: the prophets, the truthful and the martyrs. Then He says, blessed is whoever enters you. > **""Blessed is whoever enters you.""**"
"18720","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, says, I have prepared for My righteous servants what no eye has seen, no ear has heard, and what has never crossed the heart of a mortal. > **""I have prepared for My righteous servants what no eye has seen, no ear has heard, and what has never crossed the heart of a mortal.""**"
"18721","With the same chain he said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he never saw the like of Paradise while its seeker sleeps, nor the like of the Fire while its fleer sleeps. > **""I never saw the like of Paradise while its seeker sleeps, nor the like of the Fire while its fleer sleeps.""**"
"18722","Jabir ibn ʿAbdillah, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to the Jews that he would ask them about the soil of Paradise, which is white flour. When he asked them, they said it is bread, O father of al-Qasim, so the Prophet ﷺ said bread is from flour. > **""Bread is from flour.""**"
"18723","Sahl ibn Saʿd, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that in Paradise is a meadow of musk like the meadows of their beasts in this world. > **""In Paradise is a meadow of musk like the meadows of your beasts in this world.""**"
"18724","Abu Ayyub, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that the people of Paradise visit one another upon white riding camels as though rubies, and there is nothing in Paradise of beasts but camels and birds. > **""The people of Paradise visit one another upon white riding camels as though rubies.""**"
"18725","ʿAbd al-Rahman ibn Saʿidah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he loved horses and asked, O Messenger of Allah, are there horses in Paradise. The Prophet said, O ʿAbd al-Rahman, if Allah admits you to Paradise you shall have therein a horse of ruby with two wings flying with you wherever you wish. > **""If Allah admits you to Paradise you shall have therein a horse of ruby with two wings flying with you wherever you wish.""**"
"18726","Tariq ibn Shihab, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Jews came to the Prophet ﷺ and said, inform us what the people of Paradise eat first when they enter. He said the first they eat is the liver of the whale. > **""The first they eat is the liver of the whale.""**"
"18727","ʿUtbah ibn ʿAbdillah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that a Bedouin came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and asked what his Basin he speaks of is. He said it is as between al-Baydaʾ and Busra, Allah extending me therein with a watering place whose ends no created man knows. ʿUmar magnified Allah, and the Prophet said as for the Basin, the poor emigrants who fought in the way of Allah and died in the way of Allah shall come to it, and he hoped the watering place would bring him to drink from it. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said his Lord promised him to admit seventy thousand of his nation to Paradise without reckoning, then each thousand intercedes for seventy thousand, then his Lord, Blessed and Exalted is He, scoops three scoops with His Hands. ʿUmar magnified Allah and said the first seventy Allah shall let intercede for their fathers, sons and kin, and he hoped Allah would make him of the last scoops. The Bedouin asked, O Messenger of Allah, is there fruit therein. He said yes, and therein a tree called Tuba matching al-Firdaws. He asked which tree of their land it resembles. He said it resembles nothing of the trees of your land, but have you been to Syria. He said no, O Messenger of Allah. He said it resembles a tree in Syria called the walnut, growing upon a single trunk then spreading at its top. He asked how great is its root. He said were a young camel of your folk to set out, it would not traverse it before its collarbone broke with age. He asked, is there grape therein. He said yes. He asked how great is the cluster therein. He said a month's journey for a speckled crow neither bending nor tiring. He asked how great is its berry. He said, did your father ever slaughter a great he-goat of his sheep. He said yes. He said, did he flay its hide and give it to your mother, saying tan this, then cut for us therefrom a bucket to water our livestock. He said yes. He said, that berry would suffice me and my household. The Prophet ﷺ said, and most of your kin. > **""My Lord promised me to admit seventy thousand of my nation to Paradise without reckoning, then each thousand intercedes for seventy thousand, then my Lord scoops three scoops with His Hands.""**"
"18728","Abu Saʿid al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said Paradise was shown to him and he went to take a cluster to show them, but it was held back from him. A man asked, O Messenger of Allah, what is the likeness of a berry of grape. He said like the greatest bucket your mother ever pitched. > **""Paradise was shown to me and I went to take a cluster to show you, but it was held back from me.""**"
"18729","Samurah ibn Jundub, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that in Paradise is a tree standing independently upon a single trunk, its trunk seventy-two years wide. > **""In Paradise is a tree standing upon a single trunk, its trunk seventy-two years wide.""**"
"18730","ʿUqbah ibn ʿAbd al-Sulami, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he sat with the Prophet ﷺ when a Bedouin came and said, O Messenger of Allah, I hear you mention in Paradise a tree I know none more thorny than, meaning the talh. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said He makes in place of each thorn thereof a ram's testicle, shorn meaning castrated, with seventy kinds of food therein, no kind resembling another. > **""He makes in place of each thorn thereof a testicle of a shorn ram, with seventy kinds of food therein, no kind resembling another.""**"
"18731","Thawban, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that when a man plucks a fruit of Paradise, another returns in its place. > **""When a man plucks a fruit of Paradise, another returns in its place.""**"
"18732","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that in Paradise is a tree under whose shade a swift rider travels a hundred years, and its leaf covers Paradise. > **""In Paradise is a tree under whose shade a rider travels a hundred years, and its leaf covers Paradise.""**"
"18733","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the birds of Paradise are like Bukht camels grazing among the trees of Paradise. Abu Bakr said, O Messenger of Allah, these must be blissful birds. The Prophet said their eaters are more blissful than them, saying it thrice, and he hoped to be of those who eat thereof, O Abu Bakr. > **""The birds of Paradise are like Bukht camels, and their eaters are more blissful than them.""**"
"18734","ʿAbdullah ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that one looks at a bird in Paradise and desires it, and it comes roasted before him. > **""One looks at a bird in Paradise and desires it, and it comes roasted before him.""**"
"18735","Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that a Bedouin came to the Prophet ﷺ and asked whether their garments in Paradise they weave with their hands, so the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ laughed. The Bedouin said, why do you laugh at us, a coarse one asking a learned one. The Prophet ﷺ said, you spoke the truth, O Bedouin, but they are fruits. In another wording the Bedouin said, at what do you laugh, at an ignorant one asking a learned one, and the Prophet ﷺ said, nay, but they are created a creation, or the fruits of the people of Paradise split open therewith. In another wording the Prophet ﷺ said, at what do you laugh, at an ignorant one asking a learned one. Nay, O Bedouin, but the fruits of Paradise split open therewith. > **""You spoke the truth, O Bedouin, but they are fruits.""**"
"18736","ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that another stood and asked, O Messenger of Allah, inform us of the garments of the people of Paradise, created already or woven. Some of the people laughed, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, at what do you laugh, at an ignorant one asking a learned one, where is the asker. He said, here I am, O Messenger of Allah. He said, the fruits of Paradise split open therewith. > **""The fruits of Paradise split open therewith.""**"
"18737","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the place of a whip in Paradise is better than this world and what is therein. > **""The place of a whip in Paradise is better than this world and what is therein.""**"
"18738","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the place of a whip in Paradise is better than what is between heaven and earth. > **""The place of a whip in Paradise is better than what is between heaven and earth.""**"
"18739","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the space of a whip of one of them in Paradise is better than this world and its like with it, the space of a bow of one of them in Paradise is better than this world and its like with it, and the veil of a woman of Paradise is better than this world and its like with it. When asked what the veil is, Abu Hurayrah said the headcover. > **""The space of a whip in Paradise is better than this world and its like, and the veil of a woman of Paradise is better than this world and its like.""**"
"18740","Jabir ibn ʿAbdillah, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked, O Messenger of Allah, do the people of Paradise sleep. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said sleep is the brother of death, and the people of Paradise do not sleep. > **""Sleep is the brother of death, and the people of Paradise do not sleep.""**"
"18741","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that when the people of Paradise enter Paradise, a man shall stand and say, O my Lord, permit me to farm. He shall permit him, so he sows a seed and without turning away each ear becomes twelve cubits, then without leaving his place it becomes heaps like mountains. A Bedouin said, O Messenger of Allah, you shall find this man but Qurashi or Ansari, so the Prophet ﷺ laughed. > **""When the people of Paradise enter Paradise, a man shall say, O my Lord, permit me to farm, so he sows a seed and it becomes heaps like mountains.""**"
"18742","Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the people of Paradise do not trade, and had they traded they would trade but in cloth. > **""The people of Paradise do not trade, and had they traded they would trade but in cloth.""**"
"18743","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that had Allah permitted trade for the people of Paradise, they would trade in cloth and perfume. > **""Had Allah permitted trade for the people of Paradise, they would trade in cloth and perfume.""**"
"18744","Zayd ibn Arqam, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that a Jew came to the Prophet ﷺ and said, O father of al-Qasim, do you claim the people of Paradise eat and drink therein. He said yes, by the One in Whose Hand is my soul, a man shall be given the strength of a hundred men in eating, drinking, desire and intercourse. The Jew said one who eats and drinks needs to relieve himself, while Paradise is pure. He said the need of one of them is sweat pouring from his skin like the scent of musk, then his belly slims. > **""A man shall be given the strength of a hundred men in eating, drinking, desire and intercourse, and his need is sweat like musk.""**"
"18745","In another narration, while they were with the Prophet ﷺ, a Jew called Thaʿlabah ibn al-Harith came and said, peace upon you, O Muhammad, and he said, and upon you. The Jew said, do you claim there is food, drink and wives in Paradise. The Prophet ﷺ said yes, do you believe in the tree of musk. He said yes. He said, do you find it in your book. He said yes. He said, urine and impurity are sweat flowing from beneath their locks to their feet as musk. > **""Urine and impurity are sweat flowing from beneath their locks to their feet as musk.""**"
"18746","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that it was said, O Messenger of Allah, shall we consummate with our women in Paradise as we consummate with them in this world. He said, by the One in Whose Hand is the soul of Muhammad, a man shall consummate in a single morning with a hundred virgins. > **""By the One in Whose Hand is the soul of Muhammad, a man shall consummate in a single morning with a hundred virgins.""**"
"18747","Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked whether the people of Paradise marry. He said yes, with a member that never tires and a desire that never ceases, thrust upon thrust. > **""Yes, with a member that never tires and a desire that never ceases.""**"
"18748","In another narration, but no semen and no death. > **""But no semen and no death.""**"
"18749","In another narration, when asked whether the people of Paradise marry, he said yes, and they eat and drink. > **""Yes, and they eat and drink.""**"
"18750","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ was asked whether the people of Paradise touch their wives. He said yes, with a member that never tires, a part never hidden, and a desire that never ceases. > **""Yes, with a member that never tires and a desire that never ceases.""**"
"18751","In another narration with him and with al-Tabarani, it was said, O Messenger of Allah, shall we consummate with our women in Paradise. He said, yes, by the One in Whose Hand is my soul, a man shall consummate in a single day with a hundred virgins. > **""Yes, by the One in Whose Hand is my soul, a man shall consummate in a single day with a hundred virgins.""**"
"18752","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that the servant is married in Paradise to seventy wives. It was said, O Messenger of Allah, can he bear it. He said he is given the strength of a hundred. > **""The servant is married in Paradise to seventy wives, and he is given the strength of a hundred.""**"
"18753","Abu Saʿid al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that when the people of Paradise consummate with their women, they return virgins. > **""When the people of Paradise consummate with their women, they return virgins.""**"
"18754","Saʿid ibn ʿAmir ibn Hidyam, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying that if a woman of the women of Paradise looked down upon the people of earth, she would fill the earth with the scent of musk and efface the light of the sun and moon. > **""If a woman of the women of Paradise looked down upon the earth, she would fill it with the scent of musk and efface the light of the sun and moon.""**"
"18755","Umm Salamah, wife of the Prophet ﷺ, may Allah be pleased with her, reports that she asked, O Messenger of Allah, inform me of the words of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, wide-eyed maidens. He said fair, large-eyed, the eyelash of the maiden like the wing of an eagle. She asked of His words, as though rubies and coral. He said their purity like the purity of pearls in shells untouched by hands. She asked of His words, therein good and beautiful. He said good in character, beautiful in face. She asked of His words, as though sheltered eggs. He said their delicacy like the delicacy of the skin inside the egg next to the shell. She asked of His words, loving, of equal age. He said those seized in this world old, bleary, grey, Allah creating them after old age and making them virgins, loving, beloved, of one birth. She asked whether the women of this world are better or the wide-eyed maidens. He said the women of this world are better than the wide-eyed maidens as the outer garment over the lining. She asked why. He said by their prayer and fasting for Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, Allah clothing their faces with light and their bodies with silk, white in colour, green in dress, yellow in adornment, their censers pearls and their combs gold, saying, are we not the eternal who never die, the blissful who never despair, the resident who never depart, the content who never anger, blessed is whoever we are for and who is for us. She asked, a woman of us marries two, three or four husbands in this world, then dies and enters Paradise with them entering with her, whose wife shall she be. He said, O Umm Salamah, she is given choice and chooses the best of them in character, saying, O my Lord, this was best with me in character in this world so marry me to him. O Umm Salamah, good character has taken the good of this world and the Hereafter. > **""The women of this world are better than the wide-eyed maidens as the outer garment over the lining.""**"
"18756","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ narrated to him that Jibril, peace be upon him, narrated that a man shall enter upon a maiden who meets him with embrace and handshake. With what fingers does he touch her, had part of her finger appeared its light would overcome the light of the sun and moon, and had a lock of her hair appeared it would fill between east and west with its fragrance. While he reclines with her upon a couch, a light dawns above him and he thinks Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, has looked upon His creation, but it is another maiden calling him, O friend of Allah, is there no turn for us with you. He asks who she is, and she says of those of whom Allah, Exalted is He, says, and with Us is more, so he moves to her, finding with her beauty and perfection not with the first. While he reclines with her upon his couch, a light dawns above him and another maiden calls him, O friend of Allah, is there no turn for us with you. He asks who she is, and she says of those of whom Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, says, no soul knows what is hidden for them of comfort of eyes as reward for what they did, and he keeps moving from wife to wife. > **""No soul knows what is hidden for them of comfort of eyes as reward for what they did.""**"
"18757","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that if a woman of the women of Paradise looked down to earth, she would fill between them with fragrance and light it between them, and her crown upon her head is better than this world and what is therein. > **""If a woman of the women of Paradise looked down to earth, she would fill between them with fragrance and light, and her crown is better than this world and what is therein.""**"
"18758","Ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the marrow of the leg of a woman of the wide-eyed maidens is seen from behind flesh and bone beneath seventy garments, as red drink is seen in a white glass. > **""The marrow of her leg is seen from behind flesh and bone beneath seventy garments, as red drink in a white glass.""**"
"18759","Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that no servant enters Paradise but at his head and feet are two of the wide-eyed maidens singing with the most beautiful voice heard by men or jinn, not with the pipes of Satan but with the praise and sanctification of Allah. > **""At his head and feet are two of the wide-eyed maidens singing with the most beautiful voice, with the praise and sanctification of Allah.""**"
"18760","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the wives of Paradise sing to their husbands with the most beautiful voices ever heard, among what they sing: we are the good and beautiful, wives of noble folk, looking with comfort of eyes, and among what they sing: we are the eternal who never die, the secure who never fear, the resident who never depart. > **""We are the eternal who never die, the secure who never fear, the resident who never depart.""**"
"18761","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ said that the maidens in Paradise sing, saying, we are the beautiful maidens, guided to noble husbands. > **""We are the beautiful maidens, guided to noble husbands.""**"
"18762","Abu Saʿid al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that a man reclines in Paradise seventy years before moving, then his wife comes and strikes his shoulder, and he sees his face in her cheek clearer than a mirror. The least pearl upon her lights between east and west. She greets him and he returns greeting, asking who she is, and she says, I am of the increase. Upon her are seventy garments, the lightest like al-Nuʿman of Tuba, his sight piercing them to see the marrow of her leg behind that, and upon her crowns whose least pearl lights between east and west. > **""A man reclines in Paradise seventy years before moving, then his wife comes, and the least pearl upon her lights between east and west.""**"
"18763","Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Allah created the wide-eyed maidens from saffron. > **""Allah created the wide-eyed maidens from saffron.""**"
"18764","ʿAʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, reports that an old woman of the Ansar came to the Prophet ﷺ and asked, O Messenger of Allah, supplicate Allah to admit me to Paradise. The Prophet of Allah ﷺ said no old woman enters Paradise. The Prophet of Allah ﷺ went and prayed, then returned to ʿAʾishah, who said she met hardship from his words. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said it is indeed so, for when Allah admits them to Paradise He transforms them into virgins. > **""No old woman enters Paradise, for when Allah admits them He transforms them into virgins.""**"
"18765","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said Paradise is a hundred degrees, between every two degrees five hundred years. > **""Paradise is a hundred degrees, between every two degrees five hundred years.""**"
"18766","Abu Malik al-Ashʿari, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that in Paradise is a chamber whose outside is seen from its inside and its inside from its outside, prepared by Allah for whoever feeds food, softens speech, fasts continually, and prays while people sleep. > **""In Paradise is a chamber whose outside is seen from its inside and its inside from its outside, for whoever feeds food, softens speech, fasts continually, and prays while people sleep.""**"
"18767","ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that in Paradise is a chamber whose outside is seen from its inside and its inside from its outside. Abu Musa al-Ashʿari asked for whom, O Messenger of Allah. He said for whoever softens speech, feeds food, and stands for Allah by night while people sleep. > **""For whoever softens speech, feeds food, and stands for Allah by night while people sleep.""**"
"18768","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that a man came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asking, and the Prophet ﷺ said ask and seek understanding. He said, O Messenger of Allah, you are favoured over us in form, colour and prophethood, what if I believe as you believe and do as you do, shall I be with you in Paradise. He said yes. Then the Prophet ﷺ said, by the One in Whose Hand is my soul, the whiteness of the black shall be seen in Paradise from a thousand years. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said whoever says there is no god but Allah, a covenant is written for him with Allah, and whoever says glory be to Allah and with His praise, a hundred and twenty-four thousand good deeds are written for him. A man asked how they perish after this, O Messenger of Allah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said a man shall come on the Day of Resurrection with deeds that would weigh a mountain, then a blessing of Allah stands and nearly exhausts it all unless Allah extends with His mercy, and this chapter was revealed: Has there come upon man a time when he was not a thing mentioned, until His saying, bliss and great dominion. The Abyssinian asked, shall my eyes see your eyes in Paradise. The Prophet ﷺ said yes, so the Abyssinian wept until his soul overflowed. Ibn ʿUmar said he saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ lowering him into his grave with his hand. > **""By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, the whiteness of the black shall be seen in Paradise from a thousand years.""**"
"18769","ʿImran ibn Husayn and Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with them both, report that they asked the Prophet ﷺ about His words, Exalted is He, and pleasant dwellings in the Gardens of Eden. He said a palace in Paradise of pearl, therein seventy houses of red ruby, in each house seventy rooms of green emerald, in each room seventy beds, upon each bed seventy spreads of every colour, upon each spread a woman, in each house seventy tables, upon each table seventy kinds of food, in each house seventy servants male and female, and the believer is given strength to cover all that in a single morning. > **""A palace in Paradise of pearl, therein seventy houses of red ruby, and the believer is given strength to cover all that in a single morning.""**"
"18770","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that when the people of Paradise enter Paradise they long for their brethren, so the couch of one comes until facing the couch of another, conversing, each weeping, recalling what they were in in this world. One says to his companion, O so-and-so, do you know on which day Allah forgave us, the day we were in such and such place and supplicated, so Allah forgave us. > **""O so-and-so, do you know on which day Allah forgave us, the day we were in such and such place and supplicated, so Allah forgave us.""**"
"18771","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said Jibril, peace be upon him, came to him with a white mirror wherein a black speck. He asked, what is this, O Jibril. He said this is Friday, your Lord presents it that it be a festival for you and your people after you, you first with the Jews and Christians after you. He asked what is in it for them. He said therein is good, therein an hour whoever supplicates his Lord therein for good decreed for him is given it, and what is not decreed is stored for him greater, or seeks refuge therein from an ordained evil is protected from greater. He asked what is this black speck therein. He said it is the Hour occurring on Friday, master of days with them, called in the Hereafter the Day of Increase. He asked why they call it the Day of Increase. He said his Lord, the Mighty and Majestic, made in Paradise a wide valley of white musk, and when Friday comes He, Blessed and Exalted is He, descends from the highest upon His Kursi until the Kursi is ringed with pulpits of light, the prophets come and sit thereupon, then the pulpits ringed with chairs of gold, then the truthful and martyrs come and sit thereupon, then the people of Paradise come and sit upon the dunes. He, Blessed and Exalted is He, manifests to them so they look upon His Face, saying, I am Who kept My promise to you and completed My blessing upon you, this is the abode of My honour so ask Me. They ask Him contentment, and Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, says, My contentment lodged you in My abode and granted you My honour, so ask Me. They ask until their desires end, then He opens for them then what no eye has seen, no ear has heard, and what has never crossed the heart of a mortal, until the measure of people's dispersal on Friday. Then He, Blessed and Exalted is He, ascends upon His Kursi with the martyrs and truthful ascending with Him, and the people of the chambers return to their chambers, white pearls without crack or flaw, or red rubies or green peridots, wherefrom their chambers, their ever-flowing doors, their hanging rivers, their fruits, their wives and servants. They need nothing as they need Friday, to increase therein in honour and in looking upon His Face, Blessed and Exalted is He, wherefore it is called the Day of Increase. > **""I am Who kept My promise to you and completed My blessing upon you, this is the abode of My honour so ask Me.""**"
"18772","Hudhayfah, meaning Ibn al-Yaman, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said Jibril, peace be upon him, came to him with in his palm like a mirror with a black glint in its middle. He asked, O Jibril, what is this. He said this is the world, its clarity and beauty. He asked what is this black glint. He said this is Friday. He asked what is Friday. He said a mighty day of the days of your Lord, mentioning its honour, merit and name in the Hereafter, for when Allah settles the people of Paradise to Paradise and the people of the Fire to the Fire, with neither night nor day there, Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, knows the measure of those hours. When Friday comes at the time of Friday when its people go out to it, a caller calls, O people of Paradise, go out to the Abode of Increase, so they go out upon dunes of musk. Hudhayfah said, by Allah, whiter than your flour. The youths of the prophets emerge upon pulpits of light, the youths of the believers with chairs of ruby, and when they sit and take their places Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, sends a wind called al-Muthirah stirring white musk over them, entering their garments and leaving their pockets, no wind knowing that perfume better than the wife of one of them had she all the perfume of this world. Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, says, where are My servants who obeyed Me in the unseen and believed My messengers. This is the Day of Increase. They gather upon one word: we are content, so be content with us. He returns to them in His saying to them, O people of Paradise, had I not been content with you I would not have lodged you in My Paradise, so this is the Day of Increase, so ask Me. They gather upon one word: show us Your Face to look upon it. He unveils the veils and manifests to them, Blessed and Exalted is He, and His light envelops them, and had Allah not decreed they not die they would have burned. Then it is said, return to your dwellings, and they return hidden to their wives and hidden to them by His light, Blessed and Exalted is He, the light lingering until they return to their state or dwellings they were upon. Their wives say, you left us in one image and returned in another, and they say, our Lord, the Mighty and Majestic, manifested to us and we looked upon what we were hidden with from you. They turn in the musk of Paradise and its bliss every seven days. > **""O people of Paradise, go out to the Abode of Increase.""**"
"18773","Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, reports, raised to the Prophet ﷺ, that when the people of Paradise enter Paradise, Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, says, O My servants, do you ask Me anything that I increase you. They say, O our Lord, what is better than what You gave us. He says, My contentment is greater. > **""O My servants, do you ask Me anything that I increase you. My contentment is greater.""**"
"18774","Abu Saʿid al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that those who love one another for Allah, their chambers in Paradise are seen like a rising star eastern or western. It is said, who are these. It is said, these are those who love one another for Allah, the Mighty and Majestic. > **""Those who love one another for Allah, their chambers in Paradise are seen like a rising star.""**"
"18775","Jubayr ibn Mutʿim, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said whoever says, glory be to Allah and with His praise, glory be to You O Allah and with Your praise, I testify there is no god but You, I seek Your forgiveness and repent to You, saying it in a gathering of remembrance it is like a seal stamped thereupon, and whoever says it in a gathering of idle talk it is an expiation for him. > **""Glory be to You O Allah and with Your praise, I testify there is no god but You, I seek Your forgiveness and repent to You.""**"
"18776","In another narration, the expiation of a gathering is not to rise until saying, glory be to You O Allah and with Your praise, there is no god but You, turn to me and forgive me, saying it thrice. If in a gathering of clamour it is an expiation for him, and if a gathering of remembrance it is a seal thereupon. > **""Glory be to You O Allah and with Your praise, there is no god but You, turn to me and forgive me.""**"
